ally stupid it's  something I learned in the Army I was at  the end of national service and  something I learned that in all these  situations keep your own dignity keep  yourself smart and the same applies  today in the sense when those people are  at home in in sort of lockdown you know  don't just get up and hang around all  day in a dressing gown or pajamas take a  pride in your appearance still keep  yourself smart keep yourself retain your  own dignity that's that's really pretty  important the other thing to say is form  a structure for the day now there are  I'm sitting here now I'm in lockdown at  the moment I've been alone for the past  we made maybe a bit longer but I'm so  fortunate  I mean I'm surrounded by my box am  surrounded by papers I've got access  through the internet I've got a whole  range of things and I can use this time  creatively which I'm trying to do I'm  actually beginning to start to write a  new book at the moment but I can use it  creatively and that's what again we have  to say well what is there in this  situation that we can take and use  creatively rather than emphasizing the  negative and that's important and also  at the moment of course some time ago I  founded an organization called a hostage  international and it's particularly hard  for those families you know who got  someone who's taken hostage and they  have no idea where they are and they're  trying to cope with a family with young  children well what is International  gives families of  I'm in contact with them throughout this  period with many families at any given  time there's something like 2,000 people  in the world 10 ostrich and there are  all sorts of people in these extreme  situations who you know are coping that  they need help and they need support and  where you don't have it where you don't  have that you have to find it from  within yourself and it is possible to  find it familiar yourself for the  majority of people teri you tell such an  inspirational tale given what you've  gone through but you know everything is  relative people will take things from  what you're saying and apply it to their  own situations at home because many  people aren't able to see their loved  ones many people are on their own what  do you think we'll have learnt as a  society when we come out the other side  of this well I remember a few years ago  a politician saying there was no such  thing of society well I think that's  been proved to be absolutely wrong I'm  here in Suffolk you know in a small  village now we are we are very very  fortunate because there was a good sense  of community on Saturday I need you some  shopping doing on Sunday morning someone  left a bag of shopping at my front door  I heard yesterday that the local church  through a charity has organised a system  of distributing meals for those that  need it and in all sorts of ways people  are responding I think the the British  people it brief at the time like this  often brings out the best in people it  brings out the worst in some people  we've had examples of that but but  overall it brings out the best and what  I hope will happen is that we'll have a  new sense of community of society when  we've been through this and in fact even  going much further than that  perhaps we'll have a new understanding  that we need to be into and in touch  with our environment in a way that  perhaps we haven't been in the past you  know we've gone into all sorts of policy  building on Greenbelt there  this that and the other we've got to  need our agricultural land we're going  to need to support our farmers we're  going to need to support our grow our  own crops and grow our own food perhaps  a new world order is coming into being  if it is I hope it's a world order that  will mean that there will be greater  justice and fairness for all people in  the world and at the moment what has  happened is in some strange sort of way  we've all been leveled we've all been  seen that we'